1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Subtitles added by Hanson Hu

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Episode 15 - Second Honeymoon

3
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:52,720
What's this for?

4
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:55,500
Just because. How's the baby?

5
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:56,640
Fast asleep.

6
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:58,320
I'm sorry I missed him.

7
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:00,120
I had to work late.

8
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:02,480
What are the flowers for, Richard?

9
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:06,880
Five years of happiness. Happy anniversary.

10
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:09,700
But our anniversary isn't until Saturday.

11
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:10,480
I couldn't wait.

12
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:14,520
Besides, we are not going to be here Saturday.

13
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:15,820
We're not?

14
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:16,720
Uh-uh.

15
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:18,560
Where are we going to be?

16
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:24,640
If you had your choice of all the places in the world,

17
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:27,180
where would you choose to spend our anniversary?

18
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:30,640
The Watermill Inn.

19
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:33,740
I loved that place when we went on our honeymoon.

20
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:37,000
Perfect! You picked the right place.

21
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:39,320
I don't understand.

22
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:41,180
You and I, Mrs. Stewart,

23
00:02:41,180 --> 00:02:43,900
are going to spend a second honeymoon at the Watermill Inn.

24
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:47,360
Oh, Richard! That's wonderful, but--

25
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:50,040
No buts.

26
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:51,180
What about the baby?

27
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:52,560
Aren't we taking the baby on our honeymoon?

28
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:53,640
Absolutely not.

29
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:56,200
The world's greatest grandmother, Mrs. Ellen Stewart,

30
00:02:56,560 --> 00:02:58,240
has agreed to take care of him for the weekend.

31
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:03,080
Richard, isn't that too much to ask of your mother?

32
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:06,040
Too much? She loves taking care of Max.

33
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:11,320
But... I'll miss him.

34
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:13,680
Well, we'll phone every hour,

35
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:15,640
and you can listen to him over the phone.

36
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:17,500
Come on, Mar.

37
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:20,640
It's time you and I had a romantic weekend alone together.

38
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,400
We've earned it. What do you say?

39
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,540
It does sound tempting.

40
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:33,520
You're right. We've earned it.

41
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:37,360
Great! I'll make a reservation right now.

42
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:40,440
Remember that wonderful little balcony

43
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:41,600
where we had our meals...

44
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:43,880
with a view of the Hudson River?

45
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:45,980
How could I forget?

46
00:03:56,280 --> 00:03:58,280
Hello. Is Mrs. Montefiore there?

47
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,460
Ah. She's out.

48
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:03,920
Yes. This is Richard Stewart.

49
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:05,900
My wife and I spent our honeymoon at the inn.

50
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:09,520
No, we didn't leave anything in the room.

51
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:10,440
It's five years ago.

52
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:13,080
We'd like to make a reservation for this weekend.

53
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:15,740
Yes, a double room, please.

54
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:18,720
What?

55
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,600
Are you sure? But... OK.

56
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:28,640
Nothing available.

57
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:30,560
Wait! Don't hang up!

58
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:34,220
Can you recommend someplace nice - someplace nearby?

59
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:43,800
Uh, hold it. Old Country Inn?

60
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:48,940
Right. And the phone number?

61
00:04:54,280 --> 00:04:55,580
555-2420. Thank you.

62
00:04:57,520 --> 00:04:58,840
He says there's another inn

63
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:00,860
just half a mile down the road from the Watermill.

64
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,040
It won't be the same, but what do you say?

65
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:06,720
Well...

66
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:09,020
My mom is available to baby-sit this weekend.

67
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:12,240
Well, OK. See if they have a room.

68
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:13,120
Right!

69
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:23,280
Hello. Is this the Old Country Inn?

70
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:26,080
Yes. This is Richard Stewart.

71
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:28,280
The desk clerk at the Watermill suggested your inn.

72
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:31,000
Would you happen to have a room for two available

73
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:31,680
this weekend?

74
00:05:32,860 --> 00:05:34,340
Something really nice.

75
00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:36,520
My wife and I are celebrating our fifth anniversary.

76
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:41,480
Yes. I'll hold. He's checking. You do?

77
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:46,440
Great! What is the daily rate?

78
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:49,080
That's fine. Thank you.

79
00:05:49,740 --> 00:05:53,480
Yes, we'll be arriving by car about ten o'clock Friday night.

80
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:59,120
Stewart. S-t-e-w-a-r-t. Thank you.

81
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:06,960
Done! You and I, Mrs. Stewart,

82
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:09,600
are going to have a wonderful, romantic weekend!

83
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:13,700
Richard, that's the baby.

84
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:22,960
Good afternoon, Watermill Inn. May I help you?

85
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:25,400
Hi, I'd like some information about the inn.

86
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:28,720
Of course. We're located in the town of Rhinebeck,

87
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:30,640
just a two-hour drive from the city.

88
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:32,720
What kinds of accommodations do you have?

89
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:37,140
For a very special vacation, we have a large honeymoon suite.

90
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:37,980
Well, I...

91
00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:41,780
Or if you prefer, you can reserve a smaller single room.

92
00:06:41,780 --> 00:06:42,640
That's probably...

93
00:06:42,640 --> 00:06:46,040
Or a double room with a fireplace and a balcony.

94
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:47,200
I really think...

95
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:52,220
And the view of the Hudson River from the balcony is absolutely gorgeous.

96
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:53,380
I don't really...

97
00:06:53,380 --> 00:06:54,860
Enjoy beautiful views?

98
00:06:55,060 --> 00:06:59,320
Well, the town of Rhinebeck is the perfect place to take an afternoon walk.

99
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:00,760
I do like to...

100
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:02,600
And of course, after all that walking,

101
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:07,600
you'll want to relax and have a delicious dinner in our romantic dining room.

102
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:09,620
Well, I don't know. I may be...

103
00:07:09,620 --> 00:07:11,600
Too tired to come to the dining room?

104
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:12,840
Don't worry.

105
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:17,680
Our friendly room service is always ready to bring delicious meals to your room.

106
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:18,640
Oh, how nice.

107
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:24,840
Nice? Our innkeeper, Mrs. Montefiore, is the nicest person you'll ever meet.

108
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:30,800
She's been making the Watermill Inn a comfortable place for guests for over twenty years.

109
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:31,900
What time is...

110
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:32,360
Check-in?

111
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:35,440
Well, you can check in any time after one p.m.

112
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:39,080
and check out any time before twelve noon.

113
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:41,520
Now, when would you like your reservation?

114
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:43,440
And what type of room would you like?

115
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:45,300
I'm not quite sure...

116
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:51,000
You can be sure that the Watermill Inn is the finest small hotel in all of New York State.

117
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:53,720
New York? I thought I called Florida.

118
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:45,240
Right this way, Mr. and Mrs. Stewart.

119
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:56,320
Well, it's small, but clean.

120
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:00,120
Clean? You call this clean?

121
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:06,260
Have a nice stay.

122
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:11,640
Thank you. I don't believe this.

123
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:21,400
Well, it isn't the Watermill Inn,

124
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:25,880
but let's get a look at the view.

125
00:09:35,880 --> 00:09:37,080
How is the view?

126
00:09:38,220 --> 00:09:38,620
Great,

127
00:09:39,320 --> 00:09:41,440
if you enjoy looking at a parking lot.

128
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:46,840
Well, maybe we'll see the view in the morning.

129
00:09:47,040 --> 00:09:49,360
Right now, we should clean up this room.

130
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:06,720
Sorry, Marilyn.

131
00:10:07,660 --> 00:10:09,320
Why should you be sorry?

132
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:14,660
Well, the rain, the room, the view of the parking lot.

133
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:17,640
It isn't the way I hoped it would be.

134
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:20,480
Stop blaming yourself.

135
00:10:23,120 --> 00:10:26,440
After all, we're here, we're alone, we're together.

136
00:10:29,160 --> 00:10:33,180
And I love you. Isn't that enough?

137
00:10:33,540 --> 00:10:34,640
It is for me,

138
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:37,180
but I wanted this weekend to be special for you.

139
00:10:38,580 --> 00:10:43,200
It is special. Happy anniversary.

140
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:52,120
It's still raining. I want to play tennis.

141
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:53,920
I want some breakfast.

142
00:10:53,920 --> 00:10:57,300
Let's call room service and order a nice breakfast,

143
00:10:57,680 --> 00:10:59,540
and then we'll figure out what to do today.

144
00:10:59,900 --> 00:11:06,600
Right. Would you give me room service, please?

145
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:14,720
I beg your pardon? Oh, I see.

146
00:11:18,520 --> 00:11:21,360
They don't have room service at Old Country Inn.

147
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:25,440
Well, let's go down to the coffee shop.

148
00:11:26,740 --> 00:11:28,200
They don't have a coffee shop.

149
00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:32,480
We can get our meals at Mrs. Montefiore's down the road.

150
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:36,760
That's OK, honey.

151
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,900
I love walking in the rain.

152
00:11:38,900 --> 00:11:42,940
Oh, you're being a really good sport about this, Marilyn,

153
00:11:42,940 --> 00:11:44,440
but I think we should face the truth.

154
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:45,200
What's that?

155
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:48,800
This is not the way to spend our fifth anniversary.

156
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:51,400
Well, what do you want to do?

157
00:11:53,220 --> 00:11:55,160
Why don't we get in the car and drive home?

158
00:11:55,720 --> 00:11:57,900
Oh, Richard, it really isn't that bad.

159
00:11:58,660 --> 00:12:01,900
Do you want to stick it out for the whole weekend?

160
00:12:04,120 --> 00:12:06,600
Well, I'll admit the room is uncomfortable.

161
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:07,000
Uh-hum.

162
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:11,000
And I do feel bad about your mother

163
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:12,920
having to take care of the baby all weekend.

164
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:15,640
Why don't we just check out?

165
00:12:15,760 --> 00:12:16,440
OK.

166
00:12:26,280 --> 00:12:27,480
Hello? Yes. Yes, this is she.

167
00:12:28,320 --> 00:12:32,320
Oh, hello! How nice of you to remember us!

168
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:34,960
Yes, my husband did call.

169
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:42,240
You do? Really? It won't be any trouble?

170
00:12:43,400 --> 00:12:46,780
Oh, yes, I think we'd like that very much.

171
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:54,460
Fifteen minutes! Thank you. Good-bye.

172
00:12:57,960 --> 00:12:59,800
You will never guess.

173
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:02,600
Uh... I give up.

174
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:07,400
Mrs. Montefiore from the Watermill Inn.

175
00:13:08,620 --> 00:13:10,320
What is she calling about?

176
00:13:11,480 --> 00:13:12,780
They have an opening.

177
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:14,440
Someone just checked out,

178
00:13:14,440 --> 00:13:18,720
and Mrs. Montefiore has reserved the honeymoon suite for us.

179
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:19,580
You're kidding!

180
00:13:19,580 --> 00:13:22,140
No. Isn't it wonderful?

181
00:13:22,380 --> 00:13:23,440
It's fantastic!

182
00:13:23,440 --> 00:13:27,520
Oh. Now all it has to do is stop raining.

183
00:13:28,180 --> 00:13:29,480
Let's go.

184
00:13:48,600 --> 00:13:53,120
Marilyn and Richard hope to have a wonderful weekend.

185
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:55,200
Hope to have, hope to have.

186
00:13:56,280 --> 00:13:58,580
But Richard doesn't like the view.

187
00:13:58,800 --> 00:13:59,200
Great...

188
00:13:59,760 --> 00:14:01,980
if you enjoy looking at a parking lot.

189
00:14:03,080 --> 00:14:05,680
He doesn't enjoy looking at cars.

190
00:14:05,680 --> 00:14:08,400
Enjoy looking, enjoy looking.

191
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:13,720
When two verbs are together in a sentence,

192
00:14:14,840 --> 00:14:21,200
the second verb sometimes has "to" in front of it.

193
00:14:21,560 --> 00:14:23,780
Sometimes it ends in "-ing".

194
00:14:26,540 --> 00:14:28,940
Marilyn is happy.

195
00:14:30,440 --> 00:14:35,240
We're here, we're alone, we're together,

196
00:14:36,280 --> 00:14:37,200
and I love you.

197
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:42,640
But it keeps raining all night long.

198
00:14:42,880 --> 00:14:45,300
Keeps raining, keeps raining.

199
00:14:46,040 --> 00:14:49,820
The next morning, Richard wants to play tennis.

200
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:52,940
Wants to play, wants to play.

201
00:14:56,980 --> 00:14:58,360
Marilyn suggests...

202
00:14:58,760 --> 00:15:01,160
going to the coffee shop.

203
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:05,060
Suggests going, suggests going.

204
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:06,920
They don't have a coffee shop.

205
00:15:08,040 --> 00:15:09,600
But they need to eat breakfast.

206
00:15:11,960 --> 00:15:14,560
Need to eat, need to eat.

207
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,400
After "enjoy", "keep" and "suggest",

208
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:20,200
use a verb with "-ing".

209
00:15:21,160 --> 00:15:23,120
After "hope", "want" and "need",

210
00:15:23,560 --> 00:15:26,840
use a verb with "to" in front of it.

211
00:15:30,180 --> 00:15:32,600
What are they gonna do?

212
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:35,300
I love walking in the rain.

213
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:37,760
She loves walking in the rain.

214
00:15:38,240 --> 00:15:39,640
Loves walking.

215
00:15:40,200 --> 00:15:42,740
She loves to walk in the rain.

216
00:15:42,740 --> 00:15:44,960
Loves to walk.

217
00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:47,760
After "love", use a verb with "-ing".

218
00:15:48,200 --> 00:15:51,980
You can also use a verb with "to" in front of it.

219
00:16:41,400 --> 00:16:42,880
Welcome to the Watermill Inn.

220
00:16:43,640 --> 00:16:44,560
Oh, Richard,

221
00:16:44,560 --> 00:16:47,040
it's exactly as it was when we were married!

222
00:16:47,060 --> 00:16:49,020
And when I got married.

223
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:52,040
Even the old patchwork quilt is the same!

224
00:16:52,420 --> 00:16:55,420
My great-grandmother made that quilt

225
00:16:55,420 --> 00:16:57,040
when Teddy Roosevelt was President.

226
00:16:57,040 --> 00:16:59,720
In those days, when they made quilts,

227
00:16:59,720 --> 00:17:01,000
they cut patches

228
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,160
from the old clothing of every member in the family

229
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:06,560
so that each one would be a part of it.

230
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:08,040
What a lovely tradition!

231
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:09,840
Thank you for calling us.

232
00:17:10,160 --> 00:17:11,780
I remember you and your wife

233
00:17:11,780 --> 00:17:13,600
from the first time you stayed with us.

234
00:17:13,980 --> 00:17:15,380
We were very young.

235
00:17:15,640 --> 00:17:17,960
And very much in love.

236
00:17:18,420 --> 00:17:19,800
We have a baby now.

237
00:17:20,040 --> 00:17:23,400
How wonderful! A boy or a girl?

238
00:17:23,740 --> 00:17:25,340
A boy - Max.

239
00:17:26,040 --> 00:17:27,220
Do you have a picture?

240
00:17:27,260 --> 00:17:30,640
Would a professional photographer be without a picture?

241
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:36,360
Oh, he's adorable!

242
00:17:36,840 --> 00:17:38,000
And very bright.

243
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:40,280
Like his father. A real Stewart.

244
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:42,200
And very good-looking, like his mother.

245
00:17:42,200 --> 00:17:44,920
Well, it's nice to have you with us again.

246
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:46,700
Next time, bring the baby.

247
00:17:46,780 --> 00:17:49,640
You see, Richard? Max is welcome here.

248
00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:51,200
Not on our anniversary.

249
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:53,220
This vacation is for you and me.

250
00:17:54,280 --> 00:17:56,420
If there's anything you need, please call me.

251
00:17:56,420 --> 00:17:58,280
I'll be in the front office all day.

252
00:18:01,040 --> 00:18:05,000
Oh, I took the liberty of ordering some breakfast for you.

253
00:18:09,280 --> 00:18:10,980
Just put it over there, Charles.

254
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:29,360
Thank you, Charles.

255
00:18:31,400 --> 00:18:33,520
Compliments of the Watermill Inn.

256
00:18:33,920 --> 00:18:34,880
Thank you.

257
00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:36,200
Thank you so much.

258
00:18:39,880 --> 00:18:42,480
Now, this is my idea of a good time.

259
00:18:43,420 --> 00:18:51,000
Let's see... hotcakes and maple syrup, with scrambled eggs.

260
00:18:51,640 --> 00:18:54,780
Umm... smell that coffee... cinnamon and cloves.

261
00:18:54,980 --> 00:18:56,960
Homemade buttermilk biscuits.

262
00:18:57,440 --> 00:18:59,840
Slices of orange with burnt honey.

263
00:19:01,320 --> 00:19:01,820
Let's eat!

264
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:04,040
First I want to call home

265
00:19:04,040 --> 00:19:05,280
and check on your mother and the baby.

266
00:19:05,600 --> 00:19:07,200
Honey, if there were any problems,

267
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:08,080
she would call us.

268
00:19:08,380 --> 00:19:10,520
She doesn't know we've changed hotels.

269
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:11,960
You're right. Of course.

270
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:21,600
Hello, operator.

271
00:19:21,920 --> 00:19:24,560
I'd like to call Riverdale, New York.

272
00:19:30,460 --> 00:19:32,360
This food is heavenly, isn't it?

273
00:19:32,760 --> 00:19:35,140
This whole place is heavenly.

274
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:47,360
Do you remember that old desk?

275
00:19:48,980 --> 00:19:50,800
Mrs. Montefiore told me that

276
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:53,060
George Washington sat at that desk

277
00:19:53,060 --> 00:19:55,000
and wrote to his wife Martha.

278
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:57,280
What do you want to do after breakfast?

279
00:19:58,400 --> 00:20:00,520
Why don't we take a walk down to the river?

280
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:02,400
In the rain?

281
00:20:02,480 --> 00:20:06,480
No. Look out the window. The sun is shining!

282
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:13,280
Now this is my idea of a good time!

283
00:20:30,240 --> 00:20:35,160
Looking at our photographs from all our time together,

284
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:40,800
I see us both changing through the years.

285
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,580
I can't believe how young we looked.

286
00:20:43,780 --> 00:20:46,400
It seems a million years ago.

287
00:20:46,600 --> 00:20:51,640
But through it all we've shared our joys and tears.

288
00:20:52,320 --> 00:20:57,360
There we are together, once upon a time,

289
00:20:57,560 --> 00:21:01,860
reminding me of how we used to be.

290
00:21:02,060 --> 00:21:07,000
There we are together, smiling at the camera.

291
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:10,240
Each day was bright and new for us.

292
00:21:10,280 --> 00:21:13,060
There we are, the two of us,

293
00:21:13,060 --> 00:21:17,780
and still we are together, you and me.

294
00:21:18,940 --> 00:21:23,680
Every photograph reminds me how I love you,

295
00:21:23,880 --> 00:21:28,760
and how our love gets better every day.

296
00:21:29,640 --> 00:21:37,100
Every photograph reminds me how the years just come and go,

297
00:21:37,720 --> 00:21:41,300
and I can see our love grow

298
00:21:42,820 --> 00:21:44,600
with every photograph.

299
00:21:45,080 --> 00:21:51,880
Looking at our photographs, I'm going back in time,

300
00:21:52,080 --> 00:21:54,980
watching every anniversary.

301
00:21:55,180 --> 00:21:59,520
The pictures take me there again,

302
00:21:59,720 --> 00:22:02,560
but we both look so different then.

303
00:22:02,760 --> 00:22:06,880
Could that really be you and me?

304
00:22:08,260 --> 00:22:14,300
Every photograph reminds me how I love you,

305
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:20,260
and I can see our love grow

306
00:22:20,460 --> 00:22:22,440
with every photograph.

